The selected joint model has mapped consensus.308989,below the SGD prefix
reference.392135 and question-only/image-only.312360. Its number/other
groups both score0;yes/no questions account for its positive consensus.
Known-majority coverage is.606742 and the vocabulary-limited score ceiling
is.676404. The joint model improves NLL over its initialization,but this
run does not demonstrate useful visual grounding or a joint-model advantage.

> Verification and limits

Six native tests execute independent LSTM state equations and finite differences
for every embedding/input/recurrent/bias coordinate,stable large-logit soft CE
and every logit adjoint,the official ten-annotator count sequence,and full/compact
model backward. Every prepared source JPEG pixel,all415 questions' original
numeric metadata,ten-answer frequencies,consensus,hard labels and prefixes
match literal independent formulas. Every metric,source prior and preview
pixel matches retained host oracles. Exact Int64 question IDs remain intact.

The public Doxygen examples compile/run,all three canonical headers include
independently,and the original three-model workflow executes. Neither repeated
photos,finite answer coverage,numerical modality sensitivity nor a reduced
fitting loss proves useful grounded question answering. The model comparison
and actual human-question predictions expose those limits directly.

> Conclusion

Visible optimizers and source-image-held-out comparisons make this experiment reproducible. The finite vocabulary and question-only/count references expose shortcuts;actual held-out text and photos determine whether the learned joint model adds value. These custom mapped-string scores are not official VQA benchmark accuracy.

> References

- [Antol et al.(2015),VQA:Visual Question Answering](https://arxiv.org/abs/1505.00468).
- [Official VQA-v2 dataset downloads](https://visualqa.org/download.html).
- [Official leave-one-annotator-out evaluation](https://github.com/GT-Vision-Lab/VQA/blob/master/PythonEvaluationTools/vqaEvaluation/vqaEval.py).
- [Goyal et al.(2017),Making the V in VQA Matter](https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.00837).
